#{[17592186284528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284528 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94fb91b9-85ca-5af1-8e63-64ced0f72975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284528 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j]\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (t+2)\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}} & =0 \\\\ t+2+t & =0 \\\\ 2t+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1)=(-1+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305224 #edb/ref 1080 "3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(5\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305224 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305224 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07d14831-aea7-5347-84f9-25f41978fb09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305335 #edb/ref 1080 "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305335 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305335 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1540