17592186045417] [17592186410823 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410823 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "236f2410-bb87-524a-b10a-c030da587949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410824 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t,4t-1⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb19afc5-d677-5f22-bac0-92428e650b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410824 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =c⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,4⟩ & =⟨-c,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=-c \\\\ 4=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain that $c=4.$ Substituting $c=4$ into the first equation, we get $t=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}-2(-1),4(-1)-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(3,-5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25cc0c4b-5364-577c-92a1-008375ee5f34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 1759218641