f 17592186045417] [17592186433164 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c3eb033-52d1-5b96-94b9-64ac07d6af16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433168 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨1,0,2⟩+u⟨4,-1,2⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433168 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433168 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a8a9f5e6-8ed5-5aa2-8980-b3e5d5b6e091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433172 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bb45a89e-3bde-5251-b24c-b1bd686d3497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433175 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433175 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t+3,\\,1-t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1),$ valid for all real values of $u?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8d1a00d-2251-5725-a02b-3b2319318fd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433175 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨t+3,1-t,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,0,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the components yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t+3=4 \\\\ 1-t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore,  $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$\n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-1,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point  $P(4,0,1)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨4,0,1⟩+u⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
